[1mNAME[0m
       shmget - allocates a System V shared memory segment

[1mLIBRARY[0m
       Standard C library ([4mlibc[24m, [4m-lc[24m)

[1mSYNOPSIS[0m
       [1m#include <sys/shm.h>[0m

       [1mint shmget(key_t [4m[22mkey[24m[1m, size_t [4m[22msize[24m[1m, int [4m[22mshmflg[24m[1m);[0m

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       [1mshmget[22m()  returns  the  identifier  of the System V shared memory segment associated with the value of the argument [4mkey[24m.  It may be used either to obtain the identifier of a
       previously created shared memory segment (when [4mshmflg[24m is zero and [4mkey[24m does not have the value [1mIPC_PRIVATE[22m), or to create a new set.

       A new shared memory segment, with size equal to the value of [4msize[24m rounded up to a multiple of [1mPAGE_SIZE[22m, is created if [4mkey[24m has the value [1mIPC_PRIVATE [22mor  [4mkey[24m  isn't  [1mIPC_PRI‐[0m
       [1mVATE[22m, no shared memory segment corresponding to [4mkey[24m exists, and [1mIPC_CREAT [22mis specified in [4mshmflg[24m.

       If  [4mshmflg[24m specifies both [1mIPC_CREAT [22mand [1mIPC_EXCL [22mand a shared memory segment already exists for [4mkey[24m, then [1mshmget[22m() fails with [4merrno[24m set to [1mEEXIST[22m.  (This is analogous to the
       effect of the combination [1mO_CREAT | O_EXCL [22mfor [1mopen[22m(2).)

       The value [4mshmflg[24m is composed of:

       [1mIPC_CREAT[0m
              Create a new segment.  If this flag is not used, then [1mshmget[22m() will find the segment associated with [4mkey[24m and check to see if the user has  permission  to  access  the
              segment.

       [1mIPC_EXCL[0m
              This flag is used with [1mIPC_CREAT [22mto ensure that this call creates the segment.  If the segment already exists, the call fails.

       [1mSHM_HUGETLB [22m(since Linux 2.6)
              Allocate the segment using "huge" pages.  See the Linux kernel source file [4mDocumentation/admin-guide/mm/hugetlbpage.rst[24m for further information.

       [1mSHM_HUGE_2MB[0m
       [1mSHM_HUGE_1GB [22m(since Linux 3.8)
              Used in conjunction with [1mSHM_HUGETLB [22mto select alternative hugetlb page sizes (respectively, 2 MB and 1 GB) on systems that support multiple hugetlb page sizes.

              More  generally,  the desired huge page size can be configured by encoding the base-2 logarithm of the desired page size in the six bits at the offset [1mSHM_HUGE_SHIFT[22m.
              Thus, the above two constants are defined as:

                  #define SHM_HUGE_2MB    (21 << SHM_HUGE_SHIFT)
                  #define SHM_HUGE_1GB    (30 << SHM_HUGE_SHIFT)

              For some additional details, see the discussion of the similarly named constants in [1mmmap[22m(2).

       [1mSHM_NORESERVE [22m(since Linux 2.6.15)
              This flag serves the same purpose as the [1mmmap[22m(2) [1mMAP_NORESERVE [22mflag.  Do not reserve swap space for this segment.  When swap space is reserved, one has the  guarantee
              that  it is possible to modify the segment.  When swap space is not reserved one might get [1mSIGSEGV [22mupon a write if no physical memory is available.  See also the dis‐
              cussion of the file [4m/proc/sys/vm/overcommit_memory[24m in [1mproc[22m(5).

       In addition to the above flags, the least significant 9 bits of [4mshmflg[24m specify the permissions granted to the owner, group, and others.  These bits have the same format, and
       the same meaning, as the [4mmode[24m argument of [1mopen[22m(2).  Presently, execute permissions are not used by the system.

       When a new shared memory segment is created, its contents are initialized to zero values, and its associated data structure, [4mshmid_ds[24m (see [1mshmctl[22m(2)), is initialized as fol‐
       lows:

       •  [4mshm_perm.cuid[24m and [4mshm_perm.uid[24m are set to the effective user ID of the calling process.

       •  [4mshm_perm.cgid[24m and [4mshm_perm.gid[24m are set to the effective group ID of the calling process.

       •  The least significant 9 bits of [4mshm_perm.mode[24m are set to the least significant 9 bit of [4mshmflg[24m.

       •  [4mshm_segsz[24m is set to the value of [4msize[24m.

       •  [4mshm_lpid[24m, [4mshm_nattch[24m, [4mshm_atime[24m, and [4mshm_dtime[24m are set to 0.

       •  [4mshm_ctime[24m is set to the current time.

       If the shared memory segment already exists, the permissions are verified, and a check is made to see if it is marked for destruction.

[1mRETURN VALUE[0m
       On success, a valid shared memory identifier is returned.  On error, -1 is returned, and [4merrno[24m is set to indicate the error.

[1mERRORS[0m
       [1mEACCES [22mThe user does not have permission to access the shared memory segment, and does not have the [1mCAP_IPC_OWNER [22mcapability in the user namespace that governs its IPC name‐
              space.

       [1mEEXIST IPC_CREAT [22mand [1mIPC_EXCL [22mwere specified in [4mshmflg[24m, but a shared memory segment already exists for [4mkey[24m.

       [1mEINVAL [22mA new segment was to be created and [4msize[24m is less than [1mSHMMIN [22mor greater than [1mSHMMAX[22m.

       [1mEINVAL [22mA segment for the given [4mkey[24m exists, but [4msize[24m is greater than the size of that segment.

       [1mENFILE [22mThe system-wide limit on the total number of open files has been reached.

       [1mENOENT [22mNo segment exists for the given [4mkey[24m, and [1mIPC_CREAT [22mwas not specified.

       [1mENOMEM [22mNo memory could be allocated for segment overhead.

       [1mENOSPC [22mAll possible shared memory IDs have been taken ([1mSHMMNI[22m), or allocating a segment of the requested [4msize[24m would cause the system  to  exceed  the  system-wide  limit  on
              shared memory ([1mSHMALL[22m).

       [1mEPERM  [22mThe  [1mSHM_HUGETLB  [22mflag was specified, but the caller was not privileged (did not have the [1mCAP_IPC_LOCK [22mcapability) and is not a member of the [4msysctl_hugetlb_shm_group[0m
              group; see the description of [4m/proc/sys/vm/sysctl_hugetlb_shm_group[24m in [1mproc[22m(5).

[1mSTANDARDS[0m
       POSIX.1-2008.

       [1mSHM_HUGETLB [22mand [1mSHM_NORESERVE [22mare Linux extensions.

[1mHISTORY[0m
       POSIX.1-2001, SVr4.

[1mNOTES[0m
       [1mIPC_PRIVATE [22misn't a flag field but a [4mkey_t[24m type.  If this special value is used for [4mkey[24m, the system call ignores all but the least significant 9 bits of [4mshmflg[24m and creates a
       new shared memory segment.

   [1mShared memory limits[0m
       The following limits on shared memory segment resources affect the [1mshmget[22m() call:

       [1mSHMALL [22mSystem-wide limit on the total amount of shared memory, measured in units of the system page size.

              On Linux, this limit can be read and modified via [4m/proc/sys/kernel/shmall[24m.  Since Linux 3.16, the default value for this limit is:

                  ULONG_MAX - 2^24

              The effect of this value (which is suitable for both 32-bit and 64-bit systems) is to impose no limitation on allocations.  This value,  rather  than  [1mULONG_MAX[22m,  was
              chosen  as  the  default to prevent some cases where historical applications simply raised the existing limit without first checking its current value.  Such applica‐
              tions would cause the value to overflow if the limit was set at [1mULONG_MAX[22m.

              From Linux 2.4 up to Linux 3.15, the default value for this limit was:

                  SHMMAX / PAGE_SIZE * (SHMMNI / 16)

              If [1mSHMMAX [22mand [1mSHMMNI [22mwere not modified, then multiplying the result of this formula by the page size (to get a value in bytes) yielded a value of 8 GB as the limit on
              the total memory used by all shared memory segments.

       [1mSHMMAX [22mMaximum size in bytes for a shared memory segment.

              On Linux, this limit can be read and modified via [4m/proc/sys/kernel/shmmax[24m.  Since Linux 3.16, the default value for this limit is:

                  ULONG_MAX - 2^24

              The effect of this value (which is suitable for both 32-bit and 64-bit systems) is to impose no limitation on allocations.  See the description of [1mSHMALL [22mfor  a  dis‐
              cussion of why this default value (rather than [1mULONG_MAX[22m) is used.

              From Linux 2.2 up to Linux 3.15, the default value of this limit was 0x2000000 (32 MiB).

              Because it is not possible to map just part of a shared memory segment, the amount of virtual memory places another limit on the maximum size of a usable segment: for
              example, on i386 the largest segments that can be mapped have a size of around 2.8 GB, and on x86-64 the limit is around 127 TB.

       [1mSHMMIN [22mMinimum size in bytes for a shared memory segment: implementation dependent (currently 1 byte, though [1mPAGE_SIZE [22mis the effective minimum size).

       [1mSHMMNI [22mSystem-wide limit on the number of shared memory segments.  In Linux 2.2, the default value for this limit was 128; since Linux 2.4, the default value is 4096.

              On Linux, this limit can be read and modified via [4m/proc/sys/kernel/shmmni[24m.

       The implementation has no specific limits for the per-process maximum number of shared memory segments ([1mSHMSEG[22m).

   [1mLinux notes[0m
       Until Linux 2.3.30, Linux would return [1mEIDRM [22mfor a [1mshmget[22m() on a shared memory segment scheduled for deletion.

[1mBUGS[0m
       The name choice [1mIPC_PRIVATE [22mwas perhaps unfortunate, [1mIPC_NEW [22mwould more clearly show its function.

